# pgvector MCP Server Integration Plan

## What Problem Are We Solving?

Right now, [rag_engine.py](file:///Users/siddhanttomar/development/flutter_projects/brain_dump/brain-dump/backend/app/services/rag_engine.py) is doing **too many things**:
- Raw SQL against pgvector via SQLAlchemy (vector search, document CRUD)
- BM25 in-memory index management (build, cache, invalidate)
- RRF fusion logic
- Cross-encoder re-ranking
- Gemini streaming
- Associative memory search
- Location context enrichment

The vector DB operations (insert, vector-search, delete-by-source) are tightly coupled to the SQLAlchemy ORM logic for the app and repeat across [rag_engine.py](file:///Users/siddhanttomar/development/flutter_projects/brain_dump/brain-dump/backend/app/services/rag_engine.py) and [rag.py](file:///Users/siddhanttomar/development/flutter_projects/brain_dump/brain-dump/backend/app/api/routers/rag.py). Adding a **pgvector MCP server** would extract the "talk to pgvector" layer into its own bounded service and expose it as typed tools, making [rag_engine.py](file:///Users/siddhanttomar/development/flutter_projects/brain_dump/brain-dump/backend/app/services/rag_engine.py) a pure orchestrator.

---

## What is an MCP Server (in this context)?

An **MCP (Model Context Protocol) server** exposes a set of *tools* over a standard interface (HTTP/SSE or stdio). Each tool has a name, description, and typed input/output schema. An AI agent (or another service) calls these tools instead of writing raw SQL.

For BrainDump we're not building an AI agent that calls MCP — we're using the MCP architecture pattern to **cleanly separate pgvector operations** from orchestration logic. The MCP server becomes the single, authoritative data-access layer for all vector ops.

